  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'The chance of a loss is lower in Lottery A, here it is only 20%, whereas in Lottery B it is 25%. The loss in B would be smaller, but is also more likely.' There is a factual error in the report: it incorrectly states that the probability of loss is 20% for A and 25% for B. In reality, Lottery A has a 25% chance of loss (-3000), and Lottery B has a 20% chance of loss (-4000). The individual thus misrepresents the probabilities. The individual considers the probability of loss and the size of the loss. They note that B has a smaller loss but higher probability (which is incorrect), and imply a trade-off between loss size and likelihood. The justification appears to lean toward preferring A due to lower chance of loss (even though they flip the actual probabilities), suggesting a focus on minimizing the likelihood of any loss, possibly a probability-minimizing or loss-aversion heuristic.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery. This requires assessing the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. The individual does consider probabilities of loss and size of loss, which are components relevant to skewness. However, they make a critical error in the direction of the probabilities (swapping the actual values), indicating a misunderstanding of the problem structure. More importantly, their justification centers on wanting a lower chance of loss — which aligns with preferring less risk or lower probability of any loss — not with preferring a more right-skewed distribution. Right-skew in this context (with only losses or zero) means more probability on bad outcomes, which is worse, but the reason paradoxically prefers it. The individual, by contrast, seems to prefer fewer losses (i.e., more left-skewed), which is the opposite of the decision reason. Thus, while some relevant information (loss probability, loss size) is considered, the rule applied by the individual (avoid higher loss probability) contradicts the decision reason’s preference for more right-skew. Therefore, the individual is not using the skewness-based reason; they are using a different, possibly inverse logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

